# Packages for the noos-os live medium.
#
# This is the installer's own environment, not the system it installs. It has
# to carry everything the installer shells out to, plus the runtime the Tauri
# app links against.

## Base live system ----------------------------------------------------------

base
linux
linux-firmware
mkinitcpio
# Mandatory: provides the `archiso` mkinitcpio hook that finds and mounts the
# squashfs. Without it the medium does not boot.
mkinitcpio-archiso
amd-ucode
intel-ucode
systemd
systemd-resolvconf
dbus
sudo
pacman
# Explicit because two pacman hooks depend on it and must not run before it
# exists.
sed

## Storage -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything the installer drives: sgdisk and partprobe to partition, mkfs.*
# to format, pacstrap to install, and the tools it needs on the target.

btrfs-progs
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
gptfdisk
parted
util-linux
arch-install-scripts
mtools
squashfs-tools
libisoburn

## Hardware discovery --------------------------------------------------------
# pciutils is not optional: the GPU detection parses `lspci -nn`.

pciutils
usbutils
dmidecode
nvme-cli
hdparm

## Bootloader tooling --------------------------------------------------------
# `efibootmgr`. The *target*'s bootloader is rEFInd, installed by pacstrap and
# copied out of the target's own `/usr/share/refind` — the live medium has no
# reason to carry it. What the live medium boots with is rEFInd's own copy,
# staged into the EFI image by scripts/build-iso.sh, not a package here.

efibootmgr

# The UEFI shell, as the recovery path of last resort.
#
# mkarchiso copies `Shell_Full.efi` to `/shellx64.efi` at the ISO root *and*
# into the EFI image, and rEFInd picks up anything named `shell*.efi` on a
# volume it can read — which makes the shell a menu entry on this medium.
#
# Worth the megabyte here in a way it would not be elsewhere: the medium is
# UEFI-only, so there is no BIOS fallback behind it, and its boot chain —
# rEFInd chainloaded from systemd-boot, reading its kernel off the ISO9660
# volume — is new. A shell is what turns "it will not boot" into something a
# person can look at. Without the package mkarchiso notes the absence and
# carries on, which is how it went unnoticed until now.

edk2-shell

## Networking ----------------------------------------------------------------
# systemd-networkd (part of systemd) handles the live medium; wpa_supplicant
# and iwd cover wireless installs.
#
# `iwd` is what the installer drives for Wi-Fi: it takes exclusive control of
# the wireless device, so `iw scan` would fail while it runs — iwctl is the
# only way in. `iw` is kept for diagnostics from a rescue shell, and declared
# explicitly rather than relied on as a transitive dependency.

wpa_supplicant
wireless-regdb
iwd
iw
# Turns Energy Efficient Ethernet off. The script that does it calls ethtool
# by absolute path, so a missing package would fail silently on every
# interface — and a link that sleeps during pacstrap aborts the installation.
ethtool

## Graphical stack for the installer -----------------------------------------
# cage is the kiosk compositor that hosts the single fullscreen app.
# webkit2gtk-4.1, gtk3, libsoup3 and librsvg are what the compiled Tauri binary
# actually links against — note base-devel and xdotool are build-time only and
# deliberately absent.
# mesa gives hardware acceleration where a driver exists and llvmpipe where it
# does not, which is what keeps the installer usable in a VM.

cage
webkit2gtk-4.1
gtk3
libsoup3
librsvg
adwaita-icon-theme
mesa
noto-fonts
ttf-dejavu

## Boot splash ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Runs from the initramfs, so it also needs the `plymouth` mkinitcpio hook
# (see etc/mkinitcpio.conf.d/archiso.conf) and `splash` on the kernel command
# line. The theme lives in airootfs and is selected by /etc/plymouth.

plymouth

## Console -------------------------------------------------------------------

vim
nano
less
man-db
which
tmux

## Testing -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lets the host ask the VM for its address during install testing.

qemu-guest-agent

## Chaotic-AUR ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The repository's own keyring and mirror list, carried by the live medium so
# the *target* can use the repository from its first pacman call:
#
#   - `chaotic-keyring` puts the signing key in /usr/share/pacman/keyrings,
#     which the live `pacman-key --populate` imports along with Arch's — and
#     `pacstrap` then copies that keyring into the target.
#   - `chaotic-mirrorlist` provides the file `[chaotic-aur]` includes. The
#     installer copies it into the target before writing that section, because
#     pacman cannot fetch a package from a repository whose mirror list it
#     cannot read yet.
#
# The packages themselves are installed during the installation, not here: the
# live medium has no use for a bootloader integration.

chaotic-keyring
chaotic-mirrorlist
